  "type": "4 marks — Describe with Example",
  "marks": "Description (up to 2 marks): economically disadvantaged people have a low household income / cannot easily afford the costs associated with sport, such as club fees, kit or transport (2 marks for a clear, developed description; 1 mark for a basic statement).\nExample (up to 2 marks): for example, a family may not be able to afford a gym membership or the cost of a child's football kit and registration fee, meaning the child cannot join a local club (2 marks for a specific, applied example; 1 mark for a vague example). [4 marks total]"
 },
 {
  "text": "Riverside Leisure Centre is redesigning its programme to attract more retired people and people over 60. At the moment, only 8% of its members are from this user group.\n\nDiscuss the factors a leisure centre manager should consider when trying to increase participation among retired people and people over 60.",
  "type": "6 marks — Discuss",
  "marks": "Mastering (5–6 marks): A balanced discussion covering at least two relevant factors from both sides or two perspectives, with specific examples applied to the scenario. For example: the need for low-impact, age-appropriate activities such as aqua aerobics or walking netball alongside the importance of social atmosphere; contrasted with the challenge that health conditions or lack of confidence may prevent this group from attending even well-designed sessions. Both factors are developed with reasoning.\nSecure (3–4 marks): At least two relevant factors identified and briefly explained with some reference to the scenario context.\nDeveloping (2 marks): One or two factors identified but not clearly explained or applied to the scenario.\nEmerging (1 mark): A single relevant point with no development. [6 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "Springvale Sports Club has noticed that carers in the local area rarely use their facilities. The club wants to develop a targeted programme to increase participation among this user group.\n\nEvaluate the extent to which understanding the specific needs of carers can help a sports club increase participation rates among this user group.",
  "type": "8 marks — Evaluate (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering (7–8 marks): A balanced evaluation that identifies carers’ specific needs (very limited time, financial pressure, difficulty leaving the person they care for), argues that understanding these needs allows the club to design appropriate solutions (respite sessions, flexible timetabling, subsidised fees), and then challenges this with the counter-argument that even perfectly targeted provision may not overcome the most fundamental barrier (the carer may simply be unable to leave the dependent individual). A clear, substantiated judgement is reached that goes beyond listing points.\nSecure (5–6 marks): At least two relevant points on each side (or two well-developed points and one on the other side), with some application to the scenario and an attempt at a judgement.\nDeveloping (3–4 marks): Relevant points identified but largely one-sided or without a clear judgement. Some reference to carers but limited specificity.\nEmerging (1–2 marks): Basic points about carers or barriers with little development and no evaluation. [8 marks]"
 }
