=== L4 Values in Sport — Team Spirit, Fair Play & National Pride ===
id: 7890fc27-30b9-4104-b2a5-96e2ca94b9fb
slug: values-in-sport-team-spirit-fair-play-national-pride
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:38:05.206729+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 25

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How fair play, team spirit, citizenship, national pride and excellence shape sport.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Values Matter in Sport</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Sport can teach people far more than a skill or a technique. At its best, sport builds character: it demands that we co-operate with teammates, accept decisions from officials, respect opponents, and push ourselves beyond our comfort zone. These qualities are not accidental &mdash; they are <dfn class="term" data-def="Principles or standards of behaviour that sport promotes and that participants are expected to demonstrate.">sporting values</dfn>: the principles that sport promotes and that participants are expected to show. The specification identifies seven distinct values. Learning them by name and being able to apply each to a specific sporting context is a core exam skill.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write all seven values from memory. Then write one sporting example for each without looking.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The seven sporting values are: team spirit, fair play, citizenship, tolerance and respect (counted as one value in the specification), inclusion, national pride, and excellence. Learn all seven &mdash; examiners will penalise answers that miss one or conflate them.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Team Spirit</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5"><dfn class="term" data-def="A sense of unity, mutual support and shared purpose within a group of athletes working toward a common goal.">Team spirit</dfn> is the collective sense of belonging and mutual support that develops when a group of athletes work toward a shared goal. It is visible when a rugby player sets the ball back for a teammate rather than taking the credit themselves, or when a swimming relay team celebrates every individual's split time as equally important. Team spirit is not just about winning together &mdash; it is about maintaining morale during defeat, supporting injured players, and trusting the collective even when individual form is poor. Research consistently shows that teams with strong team spirit outperform their expected ability level in high-pressure situations.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">Fair Play</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Fair play means playing within both the written rules and the unwritten spirit of a sport. It includes acknowledging good play by an opponent, accepting a referee&rsquo;s decision without argument, and not seeking an unfair advantage through cheating, gamesmanship or simulation. In football, kicking the ball out of play voluntarily when an opponent is injured &mdash; and then returning it when play restarts &mdash; is a well-known example of fair play that is not written in the laws of the game but is universally expected. Governing bodies such as The FA and the International Olympic Committee run specific fair play awards to recognise and publicise examples of this value.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Citizenship</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8"><dfn class="term" data-def="The contribution of athletes and sports clubs to the wider community, demonstrating responsibility toward others beyond sport itself.">Citizenship</dfn> in sport describes how athletes, clubs and governing bodies contribute positively to the wider community. It goes beyond sport itself: a professional footballer visiting a children&rsquo;s hospital, a local rugby club providing free sessions to young people at risk of exclusion, or athletes campaigning for environmental sustainability at major events are all examples. Citizenship recognises that elite athletes have a platform and a responsibility to use it for social good.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n9">In school sport, citizenship shows when a team captain encourages a younger student to try the sport, or when athletes volunteer to help at a community event. The value is not just for elite sport &mdash; it applies at every level from grassroots to international.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Tolerance and Respect</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">The specification treats tolerance and respect as a single value. Tolerance means accepting that other people have different backgrounds, beliefs, abilities and approaches to sport. Respect means actively valuing others &mdash; opponents, officials, spectators and teammates &mdash; regardless of those differences. In practice, this looks like: applauding an opponent&rsquo;s excellent performance, not arguing with a referee&rsquo;s call, welcoming athletes from different cultural backgrounds without prejudice, and adapting language and behaviour to make everyone feel included. The Premier League&rsquo;s &ldquo;No Room For Racism&rdquo; campaign is one example of how professional sport institutionalises this value.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n12" data-revision-tip="Tolerance and respect are ONE value in the specification, not two. Write a one-sentence definition of each half and then explain why they are treated together.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Tolerance and respect are listed as a single value &mdash; not two separate ones. Older textbooks sometimes split them. The specification wording is &lsquo;tolerance and respect&rsquo; as a paired phrase. Always write them together.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Inclusion</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Inclusion means ensuring that sport is accessible and welcoming to everyone, regardless of age, gender, disability, ethnicity or socio-economic background. It overlaps with removing barriers but is a value rather than a policy: it describes the attitude sports organisations and participants should hold. Disability sport is one of the clearest expressions of inclusion &mdash; the Paralympic movement insists that people with disabilities should have equal opportunities to compete at the highest level, not just take part in modified recreational activities. Inclusion also means mixed-ability sessions, disability-friendly coaching qualifications, and communication in accessible formats.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">National Pride</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">Sport creates one of the most powerful settings for national pride: representing your country at international level, hearing your national anthem before a match, or seeing your flag raised on a medal podium. <dfn class="term" data-def="A sense of pride in one's country expressed through supporting national teams and athletes in sport.">National pride</dfn> brings communities together across social divides &mdash; for a few weeks during a major tournament, people who would otherwise have little in common find shared identity through their national team. The 2024 Paris Olympics generated significant national pride across the UK, with athletes from diverse backgrounds becoming symbols of national achievement. Research suggests that hosting or succeeding in major sporting events increases public wellbeing beyond the sporting community itself.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Excellence</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">Excellence in sport is the pursuit of being the very best you can be &mdash; not necessarily winning, but striving consistently to improve performance, training habits and competitive standards. The Olympic motto &ldquo;Citius, Altius, Fortius &mdash; Communiter&rdquo; (Faster, Higher, Stronger &mdash; Together) captures this value: the aspiration to continually reach beyond current limits. Excellence is visible in an athlete who trains at 6&nbsp;am daily, in a club that invests in professional coaching, and in a young performer who asks for feedback and works on their weaknesses rather than coasting on natural ability.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Applying Values in Context: Common Exam Mistakes</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19">The most common mistake in values questions is vague or circular answers. Writing &ldquo;fair play means playing fairly&rdquo; earns no marks. You must name the value, define it precisely, and then anchor it to a specific sporting example. For example: &ldquo;Fair play is playing within both the rules and the spirit of the sport. An example is a tennis player who calls a ball &lsquo;out&rsquo; on their own side, overruling their advantage, because they saw it land in.&rdquo; The examiner wants definition + applied example. Write both, every time.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n20">Also remember that &lsquo;evaluate&rsquo; questions about values ask you to weigh how far sport actually promotes the value &mdash; not just to describe what the value is. Real sport also contains examples of values being violated (doping, racism, simulation). A strong evaluative answer acknowledges both sides.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n22">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">The seven sporting values are team spirit, fair play, citizenship, tolerance and respect (one value), inclusion, national pride, and excellence &mdash; learn all seven with one example each.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">Tolerance and respect are treated as a single paired value in the specification &mdash; do not split them into two separate values.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">For evaluation questions, sport can both promote and violate values &mdash; you need examples of both sides plus a final judgement to reach the highest mark band.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n21">For &lsquo;evaluate&rsquo; questions about sporting values, you must argue both sides &mdash; examples where sport promotes the value AND examples where the value is violated. A one-sided answer that only shows sport at its best will not reach the top mark band. Always include a specific sporting example to support each point, and end with a justified judgement stating how far you agree overall. Vague statements like &lsquo;sport teaches people to respect each other&rsquo; without an example will not score highly &mdash; examiners reward applied answers.</p>

