  "text": "Bridgewater FC is a semi-professional women's football club in a medium-sized UK town. Over the past season, the club has experienced two serious incidents: one player received racial abuse from opposing supporters, and a match was abandoned after a brawl involving players from both teams.\n\nEvaluate how far sport genuinely promotes the values of tolerance and respect and fair play, using the scenario above and your own sporting knowledge.",
  "type": "8 marks — Evaluate (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering (7–8 marks): A balanced evaluation that uses the Bridgewater FC scenario alongside broader knowledge. Arguments that sport promotes these values: governing bodies mandate fair play codes, referees enforce rules punishing disrespect (yellow cards, bans), campaigns like No Room For Racism actively tackle discrimination, and many athletes model exemplary behaviour on and off the pitch. Arguments that sport fails to promote these values: the two incidents at Bridgewater FC show that rules and campaigns have not eliminated abuse or violence; research suggests gamesmanship (time-wasting, simulation) is widespread at elite level; media coverage of poor conduct may normalise it. A substantiated judgement concludes how far sport promotes these values overall — for example, 'sport provides the framework for these values but cannot guarantee them; the behaviour of participants ultimately depends on individual character and club culture.' No judgement = cannot reach Mastering.\nSecure (5–6 marks): Both sides developed with the scenario and broader examples; judgement present but may be thin.\nDeveloping (3–4 marks): Points made but one-sided or judgement missing.\nEmerging (1–2 marks): Basic points with no evaluation. [8 marks]"
 }
]

