--- DESCRIPTION ---
The Olympic Creed, the five-ring symbol, three Olympic values, and four Paralympic values.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">The Olympic and Paralympic Movement</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">The Olympic and Paralympic Games are the largest peacetime gatherings on the planet &mdash; and behind the competition, medals and ceremonies lies a philosophy that has guided international sport for over a century. Understanding the <dfn class="term" data-def="The global movement led by the International Olympic Committee that promotes sport as a means of building a peaceful and better world through the values of excellence, respect and friendship.">Olympic movement</dfn> means knowing its founding texts, its symbol and its values &mdash; because these are directly assessed and must be recalled precisely.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Pierre de Coubertin and the Olympic Creed</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">The modern Olympic Games were revived in 1896 by the French educationalist Baron <dfn class="term" data-def="The French nobleman and educator who founded the modern Olympic Games in 1896 and articulated the philosophy behind them in the Olympic Creed.">Pierre de Coubertin</dfn>. He believed that sport could foster international understanding and peace. The philosophy he articulated is preserved in the <dfn class="term" data-def="The statement that captures the philosophy of the Olympic movement, stating that the most important thing is not to win but to take part.">Olympic Creed</dfn>:</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the Olympic Creed word-for-word from memory. Then write where it is attributed and what it means in one sentence.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>&ldquo;The most important thing in the Olympic Games is not to win but to take part, just as the most important thing in life is not the triumph but the struggle. The essential thing is not to have conquered but to have fought well.&rdquo; &mdash; This creed is attributed to Bishop Ethelbert Talbot (1908) and was later adopted by Pierre de Coubertin as the guiding philosophy of the Games.</p>
</div>

<p data-narration-id="n6">The Creed emphasises participation over victory, effort over outcome, and the character built through competition over the result itself. It is particularly relevant to athletes who finish outside the medal positions but who have competed to the best of their ability &mdash; a quality the Games celebrates through ceremonies such as the Best of the Games awards at Paris 2024.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">The Olympic Symbol: Five Interlocking Rings</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">The <dfn class="term" data-def="Five interlocking coloured rings representing the union of five continents of the world whose athletes meet at the Olympic Games.">Olympic symbol</dfn> consists of five interlocking rings in blue, yellow, black, green and red on a white background. Designed by Pierre de Coubertin and first used in 1913, the rings represent the five continents of the world &mdash; Africa, the Americas, Asia, Europe and Oceania &mdash; whose athletes come together at the Games. The interlocking design expresses unity and the crossing of borders through sport.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">A common misconception is that each ring represents a specific continent or that the ring colours match specific regions. In fact, the five colours (plus white) were chosen because every nation&rsquo;s flag contains at least one of these six colours &mdash; the rings do not individually represent specific continents. The symbol is the most recognised sporting logo in the world and is a protected trademark of the International Olympic Committee.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>The Three Olympic Values</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">The Olympic movement is built on three core values. The first is <strong>Excellence</strong> &mdash; the pursuit of being the best in body, mind and spirit; striving to perform at the highest level and to give of one&rsquo;s best in whatever one undertakes. At Paris 2024, Dina Asher-Smith preparing for the 100m final &mdash; studying rivals, honing technique, choosing the right mental warm-up &mdash; embodied this value before she even reached the start line.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n11">The second is <strong>Respect</strong> &mdash; respect for oneself, for other athletes, for the rules, for the environment and for the spectators. It is shown in post-race handshakes, in athletes challenging unfair calls through proper channels rather than confrontation, and in competitors carrying an injured rival across the finish line (as happened memorably at a 2019 World University Games race).</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n12">The third is <strong>Friendship</strong> &mdash; using sport to build bridges between people of different backgrounds, nationalities and beliefs. The Olympic Village at Paris 2024 housed over 14,500 athletes from 206 nations in one location, creating connections across borders that no diplomatic summit could replicate. Friendship is also seen in dual-nationality partnerships, mixed-relay events that pair athletes from different countries, and the tradition of trading national badges and pins.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n13" data-revision-tip="Close this and list: the three Olympic values. Then the four Paralympic values. Check you have used the exact words, not paraphrases.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Olympic values (three): Excellence, Respect, Friendship. Paralympic values (four): Courage, Determination, Inspiration, Equality. These are closed lists &mdash; they must be reproduced exactly. Students who mix them up or paraphrase them lose marks.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">The Four Paralympic Values</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">The Paralympic movement has its own set of four values that reflect the distinctive experience of athletes with a disability competing at the highest level. The first is <strong>Courage</strong> &mdash; the bravery to compete, to push physical limits, and to confront any stigma around disability in public life. The second is <strong>Determination</strong> &mdash; the relentless effort needed to reach elite level, often against greater obstacles than non-disabled athletes face in access to coaching, funding and facilities.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16">The third is <strong>Inspiration</strong> &mdash; the power of Paralympic athletes to change attitudes toward disability in society, encouraging people with disabilities to participate and encouraging non-disabled people to reconsider assumptions about what the human body can do. At Paris 2024, British blind triathlete Dave Ellis and guide Luke Pollard&rsquo;s gold medal was widely cited by UK disability charities as inspiring a wave of enquiries to para-triathlon clubs. The fourth is <strong>Equality</strong> &mdash; the belief that all people, regardless of disability, deserve equal opportunities to participate and compete in sport at every level.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Values in Action: Campaigns and Initiatives</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">Olympic and Paralympic values are not just abstract principles &mdash; they are embedded in specific campaigns and initiatives at local, regional and national level. At local level: a community sports club that runs a &lsquo;have-a-go&rsquo; session for young people with disabilities is putting Equality and Inspiration into practice. At regional level: a county sports partnership that brings together athletes from rival clubs for a joint values day puts Friendship and Respect at the centre. At national level: UK Sport&rsquo;s &ldquo;Inspiring a Generation&rdquo; legacy programme from 2012 continued to fund grassroots sport for a decade, directly linking Olympic success to community participation.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n18">Schools can also embed these values through curriculum sport: competitive house events that reward Determination as much as results, or team challenges that deliberately mix year groups to build Friendship. The specification asks students to recognise examples at all three levels &mdash; so think local, regional and national when answering.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Mistake: Mixing Up Olympic and Paralympic Values</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19">Students frequently swap the two sets of values. A common error is writing &ldquo;Courage&rdquo; as an Olympic value (it is Paralympic) or writing &ldquo;Friendship&rdquo; as a Paralympic value (it is Olympic). The simplest memory aid: Olympic values have three items (Excellence, Respect, Friendship); Paralympic values have four items (Courage, Determination, Inspiration, Equality). If you are asked for both sets in one question, list them separately with the correct heading.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n21">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">The Olympic Creed states that the most important thing is &lsquo;not to win but to take part&rsquo;; it was adopted by Pierre de Coubertin and expresses that effort and participation matter more than victory.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">The five interlocking rings represent the five continents whose athletes meet at the Games; the five colours plus white appear in every national flag in the world.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">Olympic values (three): Excellence, Respect, Friendship. Paralympic values (four): Courage, Determination, Inspiration, Equality. Learn them separately &mdash; mixing them up loses marks.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n20">If a question asks you to &lsquo;identify&rsquo; an Olympic or Paralympic value, write it exactly as it appears: &lsquo;Excellence&rsquo;, &lsquo;Respect&rsquo;, &lsquo;Friendship&rsquo; for Olympic; &lsquo;Courage&rsquo;, &lsquo;Determination&rsquo;, &lsquo;Inspiration&rsquo;, &lsquo;Equality&rsquo; for Paralympic. If asked to &lsquo;explain&rsquo; a value, define it and then give a specific sporting example &mdash; a general statement like &lsquo;it means being brave&rsquo; without an example will score only 1 of 3 marks. Do not mix Olympic and Paralympic values in the same list. For the Olympic Creed, you do not need to quote it word-for-word in an exam, but you must show you understand its central message: participation and effort matter more than winning.</p>

