--- DESCRIPTION ---
Three event types, the bidding process, and the pre-event costs and benefits a host city must weigh up.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Types of Major Sporting Event</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Not all major sporting events are alike. Before examining what it takes to host one, it is important to understand the three categories that appear on the specification. Getting these right is a common mark-scoring opportunity &mdash; and a common source of lost marks when students muddle the definitions.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write down all three event types with one example of each. Check: do you have the correct example for each category?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The three types of major sporting event are: <strong>one-off</strong> (held in a host city once in a generation &mdash; e.g. the Olympic and Paralympic Games); <strong>regular</strong> (held repeatedly but in a different city each time &mdash; e.g. the UEFA Champions League Final); and <strong>regular and recurring</strong> (held repeatedly at the same venue &mdash; e.g. Wimbledon or the Monaco Grand Prix).</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Misconception: Which Event Fits Which Category?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n4">Students frequently swap the categories. Here is the clearest way to remember them:
    <strong>One-off</strong> &mdash; the same city is unlikely to host again in your lifetime (Paris previously hosted the summer Olympics in 1900 and 1924, returning in 2024 after a hundred-year gap from its last hosting). <strong>Regular</strong> &mdash; the event happens every year or two years, but in a <em>different</em> city: the Champions League Final has been held in Madrid, Istanbul, Paris and Wembley in recent seasons. <strong>Regular and recurring</strong> &mdash; same event, same location, every year: Wimbledon at the All England Club, the British Grand Prix at Silverstone. Always anchor each category to its named spec example in an exam answer.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">The Nature of Participants and Spectators</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Major events draw participants and spectators from very different backgrounds depending on their type. A <dfn class="term" data-def="A major sporting event held in a single host city on a once-in-a-generation basis, such as the Olympic or Paralympic Games.">one-off event</dfn> like the Olympics attracts athletes from every nation on Earth and millions of global tourists &mdash; the 2024 Paris Games sold over 9 million tickets. A <dfn class="term" data-def="A major sporting event held regularly at a different host city each time, such as the UEFA Champions League Final.">regular event</dfn> like the Champions League Final has a smaller but intensely passionate fanbase that travels with the finalists. A <dfn class="term" data-def="A major sporting event held every year at the same venue, such as Wimbledon or the Monaco Grand Prix.">regular and recurring event</dfn> like Wimbledon draws a stable, often high-income, domestic and international audience to its permanent home in south London every June and July.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Why Host a Major Event? The Bidding Process</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Cities and nations <em>bid</em> to host major events. For the Olympic Games, the <strong>International Olympic Committee (IOC)</strong> assesses bids from candidate cities. For the FIFA World Cup, <strong>FIFA</strong> makes the award to a national federation. Bidding requires submitting a detailed proposal covering: stadium and venue capacity, athlete village accommodation, transport links, security plans, broadcasting infrastructure, accommodation for spectators, and plans for what happens to facilities after the event &mdash; the <strong>legacy</strong> plan.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">The bidding process is expensive in itself. Major bid campaigns can cost tens of millions of pounds before a single ticket is sold. Cities that lose their bid have spent that money for nothing, which is why many cities are now reluctant to enter the race. Several candidate cities withdrew from the 2024 Olympic bid before Paris and Los Angeles were selected for 2024 and 2028 respectively &mdash; concerns about cost and public opposition were the main reasons cited.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n10" data-revision-tip="Close this and explain to someone: what four things does a bid document need to cover? Then add one reason why a city might decide NOT to bid.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>A successful Olympic or World Cup bid must address: venue and infrastructure; transport; security; accommodation; legacy planning; and financial viability. The IOC (for the Olympics) or FIFA (for the World Cup) evaluates bids against these criteria before awarding the event.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Pre-Event Positives</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Even before the opening ceremony, hosting a major event delivers tangible benefits. <strong>Infrastructure development</strong> begins years in advance: new roads, rail lines, airports, and broadband networks are built or upgraded to meet event requirements. These improvements benefit local residents long after the event ends. <strong>Construction employment</strong> surges as stadiums, athlete villages, and media centres are built &mdash; providing thousands of jobs in an often short space of time.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13"><strong>Commercial investment</strong> flows into the host city as businesses respond to the opportunity of an arriving global audience. Hotels, restaurants and retail all benefit from advance bookings. The host nation also gains international <strong>profile and prestige</strong> &mdash; the Paris 2024 Games showcased France on the world stage, with the opening ceremony along the Seine attracting a global television audience estimated at 1.5 billion viewers. Tourism in the months and years following the event can also increase, as the host city gains new visibility.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Pre-Event Negatives to Weigh Up</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">Not everyone is delighted when their city wins a major event bid. <strong>Cost overruns</strong> are almost universal in Olympic host cities: the Paris 2024 organising committee budget reached around &euro;4.48 billion, while total French state spending including infrastructure and security came to roughly &euro;6.6 billion according to the French Court of Auditors&rsquo; 2025 report. Taxpayers bear most of this gap. <strong>Displacement</strong> is another concern: building venues and athlete villages often requires demolishing housing in less affluent areas, with residents relocated &mdash; sometimes with inadequate compensation. Local communities may face years of construction noise, disrupted roads and reduced access to their own neighbourhood. <strong>Political opposition</strong> can also mobilise: residents may vote or campaign against the event, causing friction between government ambitions and public sentiment.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n16">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">Major sporting events fall into three types: one-off (e.g. Olympics, one city per generation), regular (e.g. Champions League Final, different city each time), and regular and recurring (e.g. Wimbledon, same venue each year).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Bidding requires covering venues, transport, security, accommodation, finance and legacy. The IOC awards the Olympics; FIFA awards the World Cup. Bids are expensive even for losing cities.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Pre-event positives include infrastructure investment, construction jobs, commercial activity and international profile. Negatives include budget overruns, community displacement and political opposition.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n15">This topic uses &lsquo;describe&rsquo; and &lsquo;explain&rsquo; command words frequently. When asked to describe an event type, always give the definition <strong>and</strong> a named example &mdash; the mark scheme almost always requires both. For a &lsquo;discuss&rsquo; or &lsquo;evaluate&rsquo; question on hosting decisions, remember to cover <strong>pre-event</strong> impacts (positive and negative) separately from <strong>during-event</strong> and <strong>post-event legacy</strong> impacts &mdash; mixing them up suggests you have not structured your answer carefully. When you name a cost figure (such as the Paris 2024 budget), make sure it is attached to a clear positive or negative point rather than just a statistic floating in your answer.</p>

