--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Don&rsquo;t People Participate?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Even when sport is on offer, many people never take part &mdash; or stop taking part. A <dfn class="term" data-def="A factor that prevents or reduces an individual's ability or willingness to participate in sport and physical activity.">barrier to participation</dfn> is anything that gets in the way of someone being active. Understanding barriers is essential for sports clubs, leisure centres and national organisations because solving the wrong problem wastes resources. Identifying the right barrier for the right user group is the first step to removing it.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the nine barriers from memory. Then pick two and name one matched solution for each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The nine barriers to participation are: employment and unemployment, family commitments, lack of disposable income, lack of transport, lack of role models, lack of activity provision, lack of awareness, media coverage (gender and ethnicity), and personal factors such as low self-esteem or perceived risk.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Employment and Unemployment</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">People in full-time employment often struggle to find time for sport around working hours, especially shift workers whose patterns change week to week. <dfn class="term" data-def="People who do not have paid work and therefore have limited household income, which can restrict their access to sport.">Unemployed people</dfn> face the opposite problem &mdash; time may be available but money is scarce. For both groups, sport that is free or very low-cost and offered at flexible times is most likely to work. A solution for full-time workers might be a leisure centre that opens at 6&nbsp;am and offers 45-minute express sessions.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">Family Commitments</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Parenting young children, caring for elderly relatives, or running a household all compete with time for sport. Single parents and carers are particularly affected because they often have no one to share these responsibilities with. Solutions include &lsquo;buggy fitness&rsquo; classes where children attend alongside parents, family membership schemes, and on-site cr&egrave;che provision at leisure centres. Creche availability is frequently cited by mothers as the single most important factor in their ability to exercise.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Lack of Disposable Income</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8"><dfn class="term" data-def="The money a person has left over after paying for essential living costs such as housing, food and bills.">Disposable income</dfn> is the money left over after essential bills are paid. Sport has many hidden costs beyond a club membership fee: kit, footwear, travel, match fees, specialist coaching and competition entry all add up. For economically disadvantaged families, these costs can put participation out of reach entirely. Solutions include concessionary pricing (discounted entry for those on benefits or low incomes), kit-swap schemes run by local authorities, and Sport England &lsquo;Active Communities&rsquo; grants that fund free holiday sport programmes.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Lack of Transport</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Sport facilities are not evenly distributed across the country. In rural areas, there may be no swimming pool, athletics track or sports hall within reasonable distance, and public transport links may be poor or non-existent. Even in cities, night-time training sessions can end after the last bus. Solutions include minibus schemes organised by clubs, locating new facilities on existing public transport corridors, and encouraging car-sharing among club members.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Lack of Role Models</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Seeing someone who looks like you succeeding in a sport increases belief that participation is possible. The absence of visible role models from ethnic minority backgrounds, disability sport, or specific genders at elite level can make sport feel &lsquo;not for people like me.&rsquo; Solutions include targeted media campaigns, athlete ambassador programmes, and ensuring representative coaches at grassroots level. The visibility of British wheelchair tennis players and blind footballers at Paris 2024 inspired thousands of young disabled people to enquire about their local clubs.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Lack of Activity Provision and Lack of Awareness</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">Some user groups &mdash; such as carers, retired people or people with certain disabilities &mdash; simply find that no suitable programme exists near them. A leisure centre that offers only five-a-side football and high-intensity circuits has not provided for these groups at all. Solution: sports clubs can create targeted programmes (walking netball, sitting volleyball, adapted badminton) specifically for underrepresented groups.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n14">Even where provision exists, people may not know about it. Lack of awareness is particularly common among newer immigrant communities who are unfamiliar with local sport structures, and among older adults who may not use social media. Solutions include targeted promotion through community centres, GP referral schemes, leafleting in non-English languages, and word-of-mouth outreach through trusted community leaders.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Media Coverage: Gender and Ethnicity</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">The sports that receive the most media coverage attract the most participants. Women&rsquo;s sport and disability sport have historically received far less broadcast time and newspaper coverage than men&rsquo;s sport. This creates a cycle: less coverage means fewer visible role models, which means fewer women and disabled people try those sports, which means fewer compelling stories for broadcasters to cover. The growth in women&rsquo;s football audiences &mdash; the Women&rsquo;s Super League regularly drew over one million TV viewers per match in 2024-25 &mdash; shows how media investment can shift participation patterns.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n17" data-revision-tip="Practise pairing: for each of three different user groups, write one barrier and one matching solution. The linkage is what earns the mark.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Examiners reward barrier&ndash;solution pairings. The question will say &lsquo;identify one barrier for [user group] and suggest one solution.&rsquo; A barrier without a matched solution, or a solution without a linked barrier, typically scores only half the available marks.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n18">Solutions: Facility Access and Adapted Provision</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n19">Physical <dfn class="term" data-def="Changes made to a sports facility or programme to make it usable by people with a disability or specific need.">adapted provision</dfn> is essential for people with disabilities. This includes wheelchair-accessible changing rooms and pool hoists for swimmers, hearing loops at reception and in fitness studios, braille signage for visually impaired users, and coaching qualifications that cover disability sport. A sports facility that lacks a pool hoist has effectively excluded all wheelchair users from aquatic activity, regardless of how welcoming the staff are.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n20">Solutions: Pricing, Promotion and Taster Sessions</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n21">Concessionary pricing reduces cost barriers: councils and leisure trusts typically offer discounts to people on Universal Credit, Job Seekers&rsquo; Allowance, or disability benefits. Taster sessions &mdash; free, no-kit-required introductory classes &mdash; lower the psychological barrier of trying something unfamiliar. Targeted promotion means advertising where the target group already is: leaflets at food banks for economically disadvantaged families, posters in GP waiting rooms for retired people, and social media campaigns in minority languages for ethnic minority communities.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Mistake: Listing Instead of Pairing</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n22">A very common error is to list several barriers and then separately list several solutions, without showing which solution addresses which barrier. The question typically asks you to identify a barrier and then suggest how to overcome it for a specific user group. The mark is awarded for the linkage &mdash; barrier X causes problem Y for group Z; solution A removes barrier X by doing B. Always write your answer as a chain, not two separate lists.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>
--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n24">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">There are nine barriers: employment/unemployment, family commitments, lack of disposable income, lack of transport, lack of role models, lack of activity provision, lack of awareness, and media coverage relating to gender and ethnicity.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n26">Solutions must be matched to the specific barrier and user group &mdash; the linkage is what earns exam marks, not separate lists.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n27">Adapted provision (hoists, hearing loops, braille signage), concessionary pricing, taster sessions and targeted promotion are the main solution categories to learn.</li>
</ul>
