--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is Etiquette in Sport?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="The unwritten code of behaviour that participants are expected to follow in a sport, beyond what the rules strictly require.">Etiquette</dfn> refers to the expected standards of behaviour in sport that go beyond the written rules. While rules tell players what they <em>must</em> do, etiquette tells them what they <em>should</em> do to show respect for the sport, opponents, officials and spectators. Etiquette varies between sports but always promotes fair and respectful participation.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Common examples of etiquette include shaking hands with opponents after a match, applauding good play by the opposition, walking off the pitch when injured without making a fuss, and accepting the referee&rsquo;s decision without argument. In tennis, players wait for a break in play before retrieving an errant ball rather than disrupting a point. In cricket, a fielder tells the umpire honestly if they grounded a catch.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall three specific examples of sporting etiquette — one from a team sport, one from an individual sport, and one involving officials.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Etiquette is the unwritten code of conduct in sport. It includes acts like shaking hands after a game, applauding the opposition, and kicking the ball out of play when an opponent is injured &mdash; all gestures that show respect without being required by the rules.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Sportsmanship vs Gamesmanship</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="Behaviour that upholds both the letter and the spirit of the sport, showing respect for opponents, officials and the game itself.">Sportsmanship</dfn> means playing within the written rules AND the spirit of the sport. A player demonstrating sportsmanship competes hard but never at the expense of fairness or respect. Sporting behaviour includes playing to win within the rules, showing respect to the referee even when you disagree, and putting the welfare of an injured opponent above the advantage of the game.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><dfn class="term" data-def="Using tactics that bend the rules or exploit loopholes to gain an advantage, without technically breaking them.">Gamesmanship</dfn>, by contrast, involves bending the spirit of the rules to gain a competitive edge &mdash; without breaking them outright. A football player taking a long time over a throw-in to waste a few seconds is using gamesmanship. So is a tennis player taking unusually long between serves to disrupt an opponent&rsquo;s rhythm, or a cricketer deliberately moving in a fielder&rsquo;s line of sight just before a delivery.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Misconception: Sportsmanship vs Gamesmanship</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">Many students confuse these two terms. Remember: <strong>sportsmanship</strong> is doing the right thing even when the rules do not force you to. <strong>Gamesmanship</strong> is doing something that is technically legal but goes against the spirit of the sport. A handy paired example to remember: kicking the ball out of play for an injured opponent (sportsmanship) versus slowing down a throw-in to waste time (gamesmanship). The question will often ask for &ldquo;one applied example of each&rdquo; &mdash; always give a specific sporting situation, not a vague description.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Values That Underpin Sporting Behaviour</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Sporting behaviour is underpinned by shared values that sport is meant to promote. <strong>Fair play</strong> is the idea that competition should be decided by ability and effort, not by cheating. <strong>Respect</strong> means treating opponents, officials and teammates with dignity. <strong>Tolerance</strong> means accepting differences &mdash; in skill level, background or playing style &mdash; without hostility. These values explain why etiquette and sportsmanship matter beyond any single match: they preserve sport as a positive experience for everyone involved.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n11" data-revision-tip="Write from memory: what is the difference between playing within the letter of the rules and playing within the spirit of the sport? Give one sporting example of each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Sportsmanship requires playing within both the <strong>letter</strong> of the rules (what is written) and the <strong>spirit</strong> of the rules (what the sport is meant to stand for). Gamesmanship only respects the letter &mdash; it exploits gaps in the rules for personal gain.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Spectator Behaviour</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Spectators play a major role in the atmosphere of any sporting event. Positive spectator behaviour includes cheering your team, applauding great play from either side, staying within designated areas, and following ground regulations. These behaviours create an enjoyable and safe environment for everyone &mdash; athletes, officials and fellow spectators.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Negative spectator behaviour, however, can have serious consequences. <dfn class="term" data-def="Violent, aggressive or criminal behaviour by sports spectators, including fighting, pitch invasion, racist abuse or throwing objects.">Hooliganism</dfn> refers to disorderly or violent behaviour by fans. This can include fighting in and around stadiums, chanting abuse, throwing objects onto the pitch, or invading the playing surface. Hooliganism puts players, officials and other fans at risk. It can also lead to matches being abandoned, grounds being closed and the sport&rsquo;s reputation being damaged.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>The Taylor Report and All-Seater Stadia</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n15">Following the Hillsborough disaster in April 1989 &mdash; a crowd crush at Sheffield Wednesday&rsquo;s ground that ultimately claimed 97 Liverpool supporters (95 died at the scene, a 96th in 1993, and a 97th in 2021; the campaign was historically known as Justice for the 96) &mdash; Lord Justice Taylor produced the Taylor Report (1990). Its central recommendation was that top-flight football grounds in England should become <strong>all-seater stadia</strong>, replacing terraces where standing spectators could become dangerously compressed. By 1994&ndash;95, all Premier League and First Division grounds had converted. All-seater stadia give every fan an assigned seat, reduce crowd surges, and allow stewards to monitor and remove individuals causing problems more easily. They are now considered an essential safety standard in modern football.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n16">The shift to all-seater grounds also changed the culture of watching football. Individual ticketing reduced the anonymity that could mask troublemakers, and CCTV coverage became far easier to implement. Crowd segregation &mdash; separating home and away supporters &mdash; was formalised. Together, these measures contributed to a significant fall in football-related disorder from the 1990s onwards.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Spectators&rsquo; Responsibilities</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">Spectators are not passive bystanders. They have responsibilities to the performers, to officials, and to each other. Accepting referee decisions without confronting officials, avoiding language or actions that intimidate players, and not engaging in discriminatory chanting are all expectations placed on spectators. Ground regulations typically prohibit entering the field of play, and spectators can be ejected or banned for breaking these rules.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n19">Some stadiums use &ldquo;ultras&rdquo; &mdash; highly organised and passionate fan groups, typically associated with European football. While many ultras create spectacular displays using flags, banners and coordinated chanting, the culture has at times been linked to disorder. The key distinction for the exam is between <strong>passionate support</strong> (acceptable) and <strong>threatening or violent behaviour</strong> (unacceptable and illegal).</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Why Spectator Behaviour Matters for Sport</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n20">Poor spectator behaviour has direct consequences: events can be cancelled or moved, clubs can be fined or have grounds closed, international reputations suffer, and broadcasters can lose confidence in covering the sport. On the other hand, a vocal and well-behaved crowd enhances the experience for everyone, creates a positive atmosphere and encourages participation. Examiners often ask students to explain how spectator behaviour can affect the sport as a whole &mdash; think about impact on the game, the club, the players and the wider image of sport.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>
